- op: discuss  # d351
  at: '2026-08-24T18:40:20Z'  # d351
  by: agent  # d351
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d351
  clock: 3  # d351
  nonce: d351  # d351
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-08-24, in their words: \"yes — WI-lutuh is the long pole and\nneeds scoping now; items 1-3 are prerequisites that make its output readable\nbut don't substitute for it.\"\n\nWHAT THIS SETTLES, and it is a scope decision the project had never actually\nmade: VERIFY-CLAIMS IS MEANT TO BE USABLE ON NON-PYTHON REPOSITORIES. The\nquestion put to the owner was whether that is a near-term goal or whether the\nRCT is a python substrate and the enumeration audit can wait. It is a goal, so\nthis item is no longer a P2 rust footnote — it is the long pole for the whole\nfeature outside python.\n\nTHE SIZE, measured before the ruling: only python.yaml declares any\n`module_completeness` at all. FOURTEEN of the fifteen catalogued languages\ndeclare ZERO — bash, c, cpp, elixir, erlang, go, haskell, java, javascript,\nkotlin, objc, rust, scala, swift. Step (2) of _uncatalogued_external_modules\n(\"an unmatched call is an examined negative ONLY over an enumerated module\")\ncan therefore never pass for any of them, so a clean boundary verdict is\nreachable only when EVERY external call happens to classify. Filed originally\nas a rust-only gap; it is not.\n\nRAISED TO P1 ON THE STRENGTH OF THE RULING. The agent is not re-ranking this on\nits own judgement — the owner named it the long pole and asked for it to be\nscoped now.\n\nWHAT \"SCOPING\" HAS TO PRODUCE, since the work is an AUDIT and not code: for\neach language, how many distinct stdlib modules a representative corpus\nactually reaches (the real denominator, not the size of the stdlib), what\nevidence a dated `module_completeness` entry requires per module, and which\nlanguages pay off first. python.yaml's 76 entries and\ndocs/surveys/python-stdlib-module-io-enumeration.md are the worked precedent\nfor both the format and the evidence bar.\n\nSEQUENCING THE OWNER SET: INV-juvul, then INV-rilit, then WI-nakut, then this.\nThose three are PREREQUISITES AND NOT SUBSTITUTES — the owner said so\nexplicitly. Measured basis: on express 7 of 17 and on bellman 6 of 23 reported\n\"unexamined modules\" are first-party (INV-juvul) and 4 of bellman's 23 are one\nmodule counted under two spellings (INV-rilit). Removing all of that still\nleaves both repos `inconclusive`, because what remains is stdlib — which is\nthis item."  # d351
- op: discuss  # de0f
  at: '2026-08-24T23:23:01Z'  # de0f
  by: agent  # de0f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # de0f
  clock: 4  # de0f
  nonce: de0f  # de0f
  message: "SCOPED 2026-08-24, as the owner asked -- AN AUDIT, NOT CODE. Full document:\ndocs/surveys/stdlib-module-completeness-scope.md. Evidence, sample, sweep and\nscoring scripts: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lutuh_scope_08242026/\n\nMETHOD. 475 corpus repos classified by top-level manifest; a language-stratified\nsample of 55 run through the shipped verify-claims on merged dev (@dfe3ba8b3e);\neach repo's uncatalogued list recomputed from its own survey using the gate's\nOWN functions rather than a reimplementation. 42 of 55 produced a survey -- 12\nhit the 420s timeout, 1 was killed. THE EXCLUDED SET IS THE LARGEST REPOS, which\nreach MORE modules, so EVERY COUNT BELOW IS A LOWER BOUND.\n\n(1) THE DENOMINATOR -- tens of modules per language, not thousands. Distinct\nstdlib modules the sample REACHES:\n\n  objc 127+  haskell 69+  go 53  python 53  erlang 38+  java 38+  rust 33\n  elixir 32+  cpp 29  javascript 13  scala 13+  swift 2+\n  (+ = classifier unreliable for that language, see below)\n\nRUST -- the language this item was filed about -- REACHES 33. That is a\ntractable audit, comparable to python's 27-in-one-sitting.\n\n(2) THE EVIDENCE BAR. Five rules, extracted from python.yaml's 76 entries and\nthe python survey: total-surface (every surface the module exposes, not the\nfunctions this repo calls); EXACT matching (urllib does not vouch for\nurllib.request); the file-OBJECT rule (json.load(fp) is not I/O, gzip.open is);\nthe vocabulary traps (logging covers stdout/stderr so PRINTING is I/O; env_read\ncovers os.environ); and refuse-by-default, because the wrong answer is a false\nall-clear.\n\nTHE BAR IS NOT MET BY READING, and this is the number that drives (3): the\npython audit's first draft was written from knowledge of the modules and FIVE OF\nITS 32 VERDICTS WERE WRONG -- pathlib, typing (reveal_type -> stderr), base64\n(main opens files), shlex (reads sys.stdin), contextlib (chdir). A 16% error\nrate for expert reading unaided, on the auditor's best language, with EVERY\nerror on the false-all-clear side. A mechanical probe caught them -- and that\nprobe is python-specific (importlib + inspect.getsource) and DOES NOT PORT.\nLocally available today: python (done), javascript (node builtinModules), rust\n(rust-src IS installed), c/cpp (headers). NOT available: go, java, kotlin,\nswift, scala, haskell, elixir, erlang, objc. objc is the extreme case -- its\nstdlib is Foundation/Cocoa, which does not exist on this platform at all.\n\n(3) WHICH LANGUAGES PAY FIRST -- and a finding that reframes the question.\n\nqualifying_only is `not unknown` (verified in code at verify_claims.py:2960,\nnot from a docstring), so the list must reach EXACTLY ZERO. And io_primitives\nstays STDLIB-SCOPED by standing ruling, so any THIRD-PARTY entry needs a\nper-project overlay too. Whole-repo flippability, 42 repos:\n\n  28  NEEDS_OVERLAY_TOO (third-party present)\n  10  BLOCKED_BY_ARTIFACT (producer defect first)\n   2  ALREADY_ZERO\n   2  STDLIB_ONLY -- WI-lutuh alone flips it\n\nRestricted to the four languages with a reliable classifier (10 eligible\nrepos): 7 need an overlay, 2 already zero, 1 stdlib-only.\n\nSO: COMPLETING THE STDLIB AUDIT FOR EVERY LANGUAGE WOULD TAKE ROUGHLY 1 CORPUS\nREPO IN 10 TO A CLEAN VERDICT. This item is NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT; the\ndominant blocker is third-party coverage, which the shipped catalogue cannot\naddress by design.\n\nTHE CONTROL THAT PROVES IT, and it is the strongest thing in the audit: PYTHON\nIS THE ONE LANGUAGE WHOSE AUDIT IS DONE (76 entries). If enumeration were\nsufficient, python repos would be at zero. Across 16 sampled repos python\ncontributes 243 uncatalogued entries -- 75 stdlib, 168 THIRD-PARTY -- and only\n4 of 16 are stdlib-only, all four repos where python is incidental (1-9\nentries). The real python projects are third-party-dominated: h5py 27, polis 54,\nvmaf 37. And 53 distinct stdlib modules remain unenumerated after the 76-entry\naudit, several of them the slot-mismatch family the python survey already filed\nas a DEFECT rather than catalogue work (sys.stdout.buffer, io.StringIO,\nqueue.Queue, threading.Event, typing.Dict).\n\nPARTIAL WORK PAYS ZERO -- the curve makes the knife-edge visible:\n  python  K=5:0/4  K=10:2/4  K=20:3/4  K=53:4/4\n  rust    K=5:0/1  K=10:0/1  K=20:1/1  K=33:1/1\n  erlang  K=5:0/1  K=10:0/1  K=20:1/1  K=38:1/1\nrust and erlang buy NOTHING at K=10 and everything at K=20-33.\n\nRECOMMENDED ORDER (a recommendation with its inputs shown, not a decision):\n  1. rust        83 corpus repos,  33 modules, bar meetable (rust-src local)\n  2. javascript  92 corpus repos,  13 modules, bar meetable (node builtins)\n  3. python      36 corpus repos,  53 left, long tail + slot-mismatch defects\n  4. go          88 corpus repos,  53 modules, BAR NOT MEETABLE (no toolchain)\n  --  cpp        BLOCKED by INV-zimud (filed today). Do not start here.\n  --  java/swift/objc/scala  DECIDE FIRST: the module slot carries bare TYPE\n      names (System, Bundle, NSData) and even syntax (swift $0, already filed\n      as INV-kotob). module_completeness may be the wrong instrument there.\n\nWHAT THE AUDIT DOES NOT DO: it does not decide the order; it does not cost the\nthird-party/overlay path (28 repos counted, not costed); it does not re-audit\npython's 76 entries; and its denominators are lower bounds.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN NUMBERS, made before publishing: my Go classifier\nsplit on \".\" before \"/\", truncating github.com/x to \"github\" and reporting every\nthird-party Go module as stdlib (151/0 -> 53/97). And a module reached by two\nlanguages (vmaf reaches `math` from cpp AND python) was credited to whichever\nwrote last, which put python's ALREADY-ENUMERATED `math` on python's list; the\ngate's own `unknown` is a set[str] with no language attached, so attribution is\na reconstruction -- 71 of 3,905 entries (1.8%) are multi-language."  # de0f
